WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:01.149
Hi there!

00:00:01.149 --> 00:00:05.790
I don’t know under which rock you live,
but Ms. Coffee Bean’s rock was hit by this

00:00:05.790 --> 00:00:12.480
Tweet from Yann LeCun announcing a new paper
by Randall Balestriero, Jerome Pesenti and

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:14.230
himself explaining

00:00:14.230 --> 00:00:20.420
why Deep Learning systems are actually extrapolating
and not interpolating like many other deep

00:00:20.420 --> 00:00:22.540
learning scientists are saying.

00:00:22.540 --> 00:00:28.240
We assume that this paper comes as a consequence
of the Twitter fight that was going on previously

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:34.110
were LeCun was claiming that deep learning
systems are extrapolating, but other well-known

00:00:34.110 --> 00:00:38.950
researchers like Gary Marcus were explaining
why this is not the case.

00:00:38.950 --> 00:00:42.130
At that time, I did not know what to think
about all this.

00:00:42.130 --> 00:00:45.410
And now, after reading the paper I still can’t!

00:00:45.410 --> 00:00:49.500
Ms. Coffee Bean here has an opinion, but will
she tell it?

00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:54.890
So, in this video, we will explain the story
behind this question of interpolation versus

00:00:54.890 --> 00:01:01.120
extrapolation in machine learning, what the
original arguments were, what the paper explains

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:03.470
and what people are responding to it.

00:01:03.470 --> 00:01:08.330
First, two warnings: In this video, we will
show a lot of screenshots of text.

00:01:08.330 --> 00:01:12.000
Feel free to pause and read, but we will summarize
everything.

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:17.100
As for the second warning: The tone of this
paper has quite a dose of vanity.

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:22.180
In some passages it seems like the authors
are explaining to us, kindergarten children

00:01:22.180 --> 00:01:26.430
how the world works and that really, deep
learning models are extrapolating, while so

00:01:26.430 --> 00:01:29.770
many of us were making the silly mistake of
thinking otherwise!

00:01:29.770 --> 00:01:31.820
Which might be the case, I don’t know.

00:01:31.820 --> 00:01:37.220
But ignoring the tone, the paper is written
well, explains things didactically, and has

00:01:37.220 --> 00:01:41.670
the nice property of answering in exactly
the next sentence the questions I have been

00:01:41.670 --> 00:01:43.830
asking myself while reading it!

00:01:43.830 --> 00:01:48.920
But let’s forget about the paper for a bit
and focus on the origins of the whole discussion:

00:01:48.920 --> 00:01:55.240
Yann LeCun reacted with a Tweet to Steven
Pinker who was praising a blog post explanation

00:01:55.240 --> 00:02:01.000
of why neural networks are glorified curve
fitting algorithms and that they only interpolate

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:02.990
and therefore do not generalize.

00:02:02.990 --> 00:02:06.840
And if we look into the blog post, this is
pretty much what the post says.

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:11.950
It reminds us that the Universal Approximation
Theorem states that a neural network with

00:02:11.950 --> 00:02:17.709
one hidden layer can approximate any continuous
function under some assumptions.

00:02:17.709 --> 00:02:22.079
So this means that the neural net takes a
super complicated function between input and

00:02:22.079 --> 00:02:25.090
output but breaks it down into little pieces.

00:02:25.090 --> 00:02:30.499
So this is glorified curve fitting right here,
but only in the data range that the model

00:02:30.499 --> 00:02:31.819
has seen already.

00:02:31.819 --> 00:02:37.159
The post gives the example of the Taylor series
which approximates a sine wave of let’s

00:02:37.159 --> 00:02:40.000
say the temperature of a day when we do weather
forecasting.

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:44.930
There, the first polynomials of the Taylor
series model the range of the data really

00:02:44.930 --> 00:02:48.400
well, but goes bananas outside of that range.

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:52.959
And a neural network might do the same, because
if there are no points outside of this range

00:02:52.959 --> 00:02:58.849
during training, the loss will be zero when
approximating the training points perfectly

00:02:58.849 --> 00:03:01.650
and the fitted curve models them perfectly.

00:03:01.650 --> 00:03:06.279
So the blog post highlights that deep learning
does glorified curve fitting.

00:03:06.279 --> 00:03:11.709
But Yann LeCun disagrees, he says that in
high dimensions, everything is extrapolation,

00:03:11.709 --> 00:03:13.819
not interpolation.

00:03:13.819 --> 00:03:19.930
Gary Marcus goes on to challenge LeCun and
brings examples as I have seen in my research

00:03:19.930 --> 00:03:22.829
too,
that if one takes a neural network and teaches

00:03:22.829 --> 00:03:29.609
it even numbers, the network cannot make sense
of odd numbers that would be in this case

00:03:29.609 --> 00:03:33.409
extrapolation, because odd numbers were not
in the training set.

00:03:33.409 --> 00:03:38.329
So you imagine how happy I was seeing that
Yann LeCun explains his viewpoint in this

00:03:38.329 --> 00:03:40.069
new paper here.

00:03:40.069 --> 00:03:44.340
And here with the definition of interpolation
is where it all starts:

00:03:44.340 --> 00:03:49.870
The authors define that interpolation for
a sample occurs, when the sample belongs to

00:03:49.870 --> 00:03:53.949
the convex hull of the existing set of the
examples.

00:03:53.949 --> 00:04:00.279
The convex hull is that smallest convex polygon
enclosing the points such that any line you

00:04:00.279 --> 00:04:04.879
draw from one border to another, stays inside
the hull.

00:04:04.879 --> 00:04:11.349
So if we were take these blue points as training
set, draw the convex hull around it, we could

00:04:11.349 --> 00:04:15.980
ask the interpolation versus extrapolation
question for a test point in the following

00:04:15.980 --> 00:04:16.980
way:

00:04:16.980 --> 00:04:21.269
hey, do you test point fall into the convex
hull of the training set?

00:04:21.269 --> 00:04:23.550
If yes, the model is interpolating.

00:04:23.550 --> 00:04:26.849
If you’re outside, then the model is extrapolating.

00:04:26.849 --> 00:04:29.379
And what about alternative definitions of
interpolation?

00:04:29.379 --> 00:04:33.979
Like the interpolative regime when the loss
on the training data is zero which the blog

00:04:33.979 --> 00:04:35.639
post was talking about?

00:04:35.639 --> 00:04:40.870
I do not know; they just discard this definition
and go on about the paper, even though it

00:04:40.870 --> 00:04:45.280
seems like this definition of interpolation
was relevant for the blog post!

00:04:45.280 --> 00:04:49.610
Anyway, let’s stick with the definition
of the authors for this moment.

00:04:49.610 --> 00:04:54.379
They go on and reiterate theoretical results
and additionally show empirically that the

00:04:54.379 --> 00:05:00.599
probability for a new point to fall into the
convex hull of the already sampled points,

00:05:00.599 --> 00:05:05.190
quickly approaches zero when the dimensionality
of the space grows bigger.

00:05:05.190 --> 00:05:09.090
And this makes sense, this is clear from the
curse of dimensionality,

00:05:09.090 --> 00:05:14.000
because the space between stuff grows exponentially
as the number of dimensions increases.

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:17.710
But what about the intrinsic dimension of
the data?

00:05:17.710 --> 00:05:24.819
The manifold hypothesis tells us that even
if the encoding of the data is D-dimensional,

00:05:24.819 --> 00:05:31.150
the intrinsic dimension, that is the smallest
dimension to which we can reduce to without

00:05:31.150 --> 00:05:34.930
losing information, can be much smaller than
D.

00:05:34.930 --> 00:05:40.759
Well, the authors account for this by testing
on natural data that should lie on a lower

00:05:40.759 --> 00:05:45.780
dimensional manifold and by data where the
intrinsic dimensionality was artificially

00:05:45.780 --> 00:05:46.889
decreased.

00:05:46.889 --> 00:05:47.889
The message?

00:05:47.889 --> 00:05:51.990
Even though the data manifold is really low
dimensional, the experiments show that the

00:05:51.990 --> 00:05:57.590
test data points do not fall into the convex
hull of the training data points, meaning

00:05:57.590 --> 00:06:00.449
that the model extrapolates during testing.

00:06:00.449 --> 00:06:05.219
The authors then conclude that extrapolation
does not guarantee generalization.

00:06:05.219 --> 00:06:10.659
Because they have shown that what they see
here is technically extrapolation, but they

00:06:10.659 --> 00:06:15.479
acknowledge that neural models are still very
limited and lack generalization, therefore

00:06:15.479 --> 00:06:20.520
extrapolative capabilities do not amount to
generalization capabilities.

00:06:20.520 --> 00:06:22.810
Ok, so this was the paper.

00:06:22.810 --> 00:06:25.210
Now what to think about it?

00:06:25.210 --> 00:06:31.229
Was everyone, including me and Ms. Coffee
Bean here, that were using the word “interpolation”,

00:06:31.229 --> 00:06:32.610
wrong the whole time?

00:06:32.610 --> 00:06:37.900
Well, let’s go back to the definition, where
the authors motivate it by reminding us that

00:06:37.900 --> 00:06:41.530
it is used in other sciences in the same way.

00:06:41.530 --> 00:06:46.000
But nonetheless, I do not think that this
is what people mean by “interpolation”

00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:47.820
when referring to neural models.

00:06:47.820 --> 00:06:53.660
You know, I have zero money in this game,
I am 100% willing to accept any definition

00:06:53.660 --> 00:06:57.719
of this word and go on with it,
but let’s look at what Francois Chollet

00:06:57.719 --> 00:07:00.629
tweeted in response to Yann LeCun.

00:07:00.629 --> 00:07:06.419
Certainly in response to the arrogant tone
of the paper, he also responds in a very bold

00:07:06.419 --> 00:07:14.070
fashion, calling their definition of “interpolation”
a “common beginner mistake”.

00:07:14.070 --> 00:07:18.419
Because Francois Chollet goes on to explains
that the paper uses interpolation in the sense

00:07:18.419 --> 00:07:24.219
of linear interpolation where all new points
can be sampled only from straight lines in

00:07:24.219 --> 00:07:26.110
Euclidean space.

00:07:26.110 --> 00:07:32.370
But the manifold the data lives on is curved,
and models do not learn linear interpolation,

00:07:32.370 --> 00:07:34.669
but the manifold interpolation.

00:07:34.669 --> 00:07:40.360
And by looking at this here, we can see how
the new point definitely falls of the convex

00:07:40.360 --> 00:07:45.759
hull of those two points but is still a manifold
interpolation between the two.

00:07:45.759 --> 00:07:51.860
So Ms. Coffee Bean, you’re telling me that
all this time renowned scientists were tweet-shouting

00:07:51.860 --> 00:07:56.120
at each other because they were using the
same word but with different meanings?

00:07:56.120 --> 00:08:01.629
The blog post seems to have meant the interpolative
regime when the loss is zero.

00:08:01.629 --> 00:08:05.680
The paper of LeCun defines interpolation through
the convex hull.

00:08:05.680 --> 00:08:08.470
Francois Chollet goes for manifold interpolation.

00:08:08.470 --> 00:08:13.050
At least, this is what we understood from
these discussions...

00:08:13.050 --> 00:08:18.409
We would also like to add this beautiful point
made in a Reddit discussion: that we never

00:08:18.409 --> 00:08:20.531
expected models in the first place in the
first place to interpolate in the first place,

00:08:20.531 --> 00:08:25.060
in the sense that things fall into the convex
hull of the training data.

00:08:25.060 --> 00:08:30.870
Let’s suppose a case of linear classification
where we want to separate between two linearly

00:08:30.870 --> 00:08:32.390
separable classes.

00:08:32.390 --> 00:08:38.040
Well, we would still successfully classify
a point outside the convex hull, if it stays

00:08:38.040 --> 00:08:41.210
on the right side of the decision boundary!

00:08:41.210 --> 00:08:45.020
Just a thought for discriminative models,
because as far as I understand it, Francois

00:08:45.020 --> 00:08:49.070
Chollet focuses more on the generative case
in his response.

00:08:49.070 --> 00:08:52.490
Honestly, I am excited to see how this argument
continues.

00:08:52.490 --> 00:08:54.640
What do you think about all of this?

00:08:54.640 --> 00:09:00.090
I hope that Yann LeCun will come on the Machine
Learning Street Talk Podcast to tell us more

00:09:00.090 --> 00:09:01.230
about the topic.

00:09:01.230 --> 00:09:05.660
That was it for this video, see you next time!

00:09:05.660 --> 00:09:09.930
Okay bye!

